---
.title = "Exe",
.description = "Bind device buffers, run a compiled executable, retrieve its outputs, and handle ownership.",
.date = .date("2026-11-17T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
.custom = .{ .doc_section = "API Reference" },
---
[`zml.Exe`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/exe.zig)
is the loaded executable returned by [Platform compilation]($link.page('zml/api-reference/platform').ref('compile-a-model-or-function')).
Pass buffers in the structure and order of the compiled arguments, using the
expected sizes, types, and shards.
The call forms below use a `std.mem.Allocator` for `allocator` and a `std.Io` for `io`.
A return type with `!` can carry an error.

# [Bind arguments and allocate results]($heading.id('exe'))

- `exe.args(allocator)`\
  `!Exe.Arguments`. Allocate argument slots for the executable.
- `arguments.set(buffers)`\
  `void`. Bind the provided buffers to the argument slots.
- `exe.results(allocator)`\
  `!Exe.Results`. Allocate result slots for the executable.

# [Run the executable and read results]($heading.id('run-and-read-results'))

Use either recipe with `executable`, `layer_buffers`, and `input_buffer` from the
[linear layer example]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/write-your-first-model').ref('compile-and-call-the-layer')).
Both produce the same result for this layer, which uses no input donation.

```=html
<div class="exe-recipes">
  <section class="exe-recipe" aria-labelledby="exe-call-recipe">
    <h3 id="exe-call-recipe"><code>call</code> with explicit slots</h3>
    <pre><code class="zig" data-copy-label="Copy Exe.call example">var arguments = try executable.args(allocator);
defer arguments.deinit(allocator);
arguments.set(.{ layer_buffers, input_buffer });

var results = try executable.results(allocator);
defer results.deinit(allocator);
executable.call(io, arguments, &amp;results, .{ .wait = true });

var output_buffer = results.get(zml.Buffer);
defer output_buffer.deinit();</code></pre>
    <dl class="exe-recipe-rules">
      <dt>Wait</dt>
      <dd><code>call</code> returns <code>void</code>. Passing <code>.wait = true</code> waits for completion. The default is <code>false</code>.</dd>
      <dt>Output</dt>
      <dd><code>results.get(zml.Buffer)</code> retrieves one owned device buffer without waiting. For multiple outputs, pass the matching structure of buffers. Retrieve once per run and release every output.</dd>
      <dt>Donated inputs</dt>
      <dd>The previous contents are invalid after submission. The caller still destroys the old handles.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="exe-recipe" aria-labelledby="exe-eval-recipe">
    <h3 id="exe-eval-recipe"><code>eval</code> for one output</h3>
    <pre><code class="zig" data-copy-label="Copy Exe.eval example">var output_buffer = try executable.eval(
    allocator,
    io,
    .{ layer_buffers, input_buffer },
);
defer output_buffer.deinit();</code></pre>
    <dl class="exe-recipe-rules">
      <dt>Wait</dt>
      <dd><code>eval</code> always waits for completion.</dd>
      <dt>Output</dt>
      <dd><code>eval</code> returns <code>!Buffer</code>. The caller owns the single device buffer and releases it with <code>deinit()</code>.</dd>
      <dt>Donated inputs</dt>
      <dd><code>eval</code> destroys handles for inputs the computation was compiled to donate. Other input handles stay caller-owned.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>
```

# [Release slots and buffers]($heading.id('ownership-and-cleanup'))

- `exe.deinit()`\
  `void`. Release the loaded executable.

Release argument and result slots with `deinit(allocator)`. Release each
owned buffer with `buffer.deinit()`.
